    "topic_code": "2.4",
    "topic_title": "Exponential Function Manipulation",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = 5·8^(2x−1). Which expression rewrites f(x) using base 2 and is equivalent to f(x) for every real x?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "40·2^(6x)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Negative-exponent error: this multiplies the coefficient by 8 instead of dividing by 8. The constant factor is 8⁻¹ = 1/8, not 8. Fix: Rewrite the negative power as a reciprocal before combining constant factors."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "(5/8)·2^(2x)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Base-change error: this replaces base 8 with base 2 without multiplying the variable exponent by 3. At x = 1, it gives 5/2, while the original gives 40. Fix: Substitute 8 = 2³ and apply the power-of-a-power rule."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "(5/8)·2^(6x)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Since 8 = 2³, exponent rules give 5·8^(2x−1) = 5·2^(6x−3) = (5/8)·2^(6x). As a check, at x = 1, both the original and rewritten expressions equal 40."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "5·2^(6x−1)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Incomplete-distribution error: this multiplies 2x by 3 but leaves −1 unchanged. The exponent must become 3(2x−1) = 6x−3. Fix: Distribute the outer exponent to every term inside the parentheses."
     }
    ]
   },
