   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.4",
    "topic_title": "Exponential Function Manipulation",
    "stem": "The function f is given by f(x) = 5·3^(2x+1). Which of the following is an equivalent form of f(x) written as a·b^x, or as an expression built from such terms?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = 15·6^x",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This multiplies the base by the exponent's coefficient, treating 3^(2x) as (3·2)^x. The power rule gives 3^(2x) = (3²)^x = 9^x, and at x = 1 this form gives 90 instead of 135. Fix: Rewrite b^(kx) as (b^k)^x, then compute b^k by repeated multiplication."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = 15·9^x",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "By the product rule, 3^(2x+1) = 3^(2x)·3¹. By the power rule, 3^(2x) = (3²)^x = 9^x. So f(x) = 5·3·9^x = 15·9^x. Check at x = 1: 5·3³ = 135 and 15·9 = 135."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = 45·9^x",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats 2x + 1 as if it were 2(x + 1), so 3^(2x+1) becomes 9^(x+1) = 9·9^x. But 9^(x+1) = 3^(2x+2), one factor of 3 too many, giving 45 instead of 15 at x = 0. Fix: Expand any factored exponent and check that it matches 2x + 1 exactly."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = 5·9^x + 15",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This distributes the exponent across a sum, writing 3^(2x+1) as 3^(2x) + 3¹. A sum in the exponent produces a product of powers, not a sum, and at x = 0 this form gives 20 instead of 15. Fix: Rewrite b^(m+n) as b^m·b^n, then evaluate both forms at x = 0."
     }
    ]
   },
