    "topic_title": "Exponential Function Manipulation",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = 3·2^(2x+1). Which expression in the form a·b^x is equivalent to f(x) for every real x?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "6·2^x",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Dropped exponent multiplier: this keeps the factor from 2¹ but incorrectly replaces 2^(2x) with 2^x. At x = 1, this expression equals 12, whereas f(1) = 24. Fix: Rewrite 2^(2x) as (2²)^x before combining factors outside the exponential."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "6·4^x",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Using exponent laws, 3·2^(2x+1) = 3·2·(2²)^x = 6·4^x. As a check, at x = 0, both the original and rewritten expressions equal 6."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "3·4^x",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Lost constant factor: this correctly rewrites 2^(2x) as 4^x but omits the factor 2¹. At x = 0, this expression equals 3, whereas f(0) = 6. Fix: Split 2^(2x+1) into 2^(2x)·2¹ and retain both factors."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "12·4^x",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Incorrect base conversion: rewriting 2^(2x+1) as 4^(x+1) produces this expression, but 4^(x+1) = 2^(2x+2). At x = 0, this expression equals 12, whereas f(0) = 6. Fix: When changing the base from 2 to 4, divide the entire exponent by 2."
     }
    ]
